<blockquote>
<p>The article doesn't state anywhere what SCP-3129's area of effect actually is. Is it just the continental United States? Has it appeared anywhere else outside the United States? Does SCP-3129 appear more often in places where the ideas of community and togetherness are &quot;on the decline&quot;? Or does it prefer places where those ideas are already strongly valued? How frequent are SCP-3129 infected broadcasts?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Good questions, thanks! I'll keep this in mind for the pending rewrite.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The description lead me to believe that SCP-3129-1 has specific ideas that people who already agree with make them more susceptible to infection, but the example log at the bottom seems to cite 3129-1's main ideas as security, togetherness, and community which are pretty board and popular ideas among any stock politician regardless of party, and ideas which include a pretty broad amount of potentially vulnerable infectees. Wouldn't that pool of possible cognitohazard-vectors as well as the incident report (which might suggest possible reality reconstruction or at least a perception-based effect) be a point of concern for the Foundation to at least consider a pending Keter classification?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>It doesn't actually. The commercials are vague on purpose: &quot;Mr November&quot; doesn't actually understand politics. Its use of local politics is purely opportunistic. It saw a quick and dirty way to get what it needed (attention) and decided to emulate it. Badly. This thing's poor reproduction of actual campaign ads is entirely intentional. It doesn't even need to be actually convincing because the hazardous aspect of the transmissions is what gets it what it needs. The fact that it works on people of particular alignment at any given time is coincidental.</p>
<p>It doesn't really have ideas of its own as far as an actual &quot;campaign&quot; is concerned. Different commercials say different things. The entity just throws out what it thinks people want to hear and tries its best to carry the facade of an actual person.</p>
<p>As far as its classification is concerned, maybe a pending Keter designation is appropriate. This is just an unfortunate result of the end of the first addendum not aligning with the rest of the article, as pointed out by Mortos. That's the main goal of the rewrite. Whitch doesn't have a timeline right now because I have no idea what to write for it. It's been a long, difficult work week and I feel burnt out.</p>
<p>I won't let it sit as is for too much longer. If I can't get a rewrite done within a few days I'm gonna self-delete so I can work on it without worrying about the score of this one.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>I don't wanna downvote, but I just feel like the ideas as they are presented here aren't fully developed and realized. If can address a few of these questions in some edits, I think I could confidently upvote this. Otherwise, this feels like it's almost something good but just not quite there yet.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Agreed. I have to rewrite the middle of this article. Thanks for your input.</p>

